  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$",
